\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Unprivileged nmap's run length, and therefore its recall, is set by the scanning host's kernel rather than by nmap or by the segment.} The same binary, argv and ten responders produce 0 to 2{,}387 ARP retries for the 244 empty addresses, and the wall clock follows at $\rho =+0.972$; longer runs are still listening when closed-port resets arrive, which nmap counts as hosts up. This covariate, not the privilege mechanism, is what refutes H6.}}{10}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arp}{{5}{10}{\textbf {Unprivileged nmap's run length, and therefore its recall, is set by the scanning host's kernel rather than by nmap or by the segment.} The same binary, argv and ten responders produce 0 to 2{,}387 ARP retries for the 244 empty addresses, and the wall clock follows at $\rho =+0.972$; longer runs are still listening when closed-port resets arrive, which nmap counts as hosts up. This covariate, not the privilege mechanism, is what refutes H6}{figure.5}{}}
